{"experiments":[  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"The spatial topography of the average word‐onset evoked response (0–200 ms) is highly consistent across recording sessions.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Quantify the similarity of word‐evoked MEG topographies across the ten sessions to assess cross‐session reliability of auditory ERFs.",  
      "steps":"1. For each session (1–10), load the events.tsv and select all word onsets (kind=='word') with valid timemeg timestamps.  2. Load the corresponding MEG .h5 file and epoch each word from 0 to 0.2 s post‐onset, baseline‐correct using −0.1–0 s.  3. Average the baseline‐corrected epochs across all words to obtain a session‐specific ERF (306 channels × time).  4. Collapse each session’s ERF to a topography vector at 100 ms (select the timepoint closest to 0.1 s) across all channels.  5. Compute the Pearson correlation (or cosine similarity) between every pair of session topography vectors, producing a 10×10 similarity matrix.  6. Test whether mean off‐diagonal similarity is significantly greater than zero via permutation (shuffle channel labels within topographies 1,000 times).",  
      "deliverables":"1. A 10×10 session‐by‐session similarity matrix of ERF topographies at 100 ms.  2. Distribution of null similarities from permutations and corresponding p‐value for observed mean similarity.  3. Heatmap visualization of session similarity matrix, annotated with significance threshold.  4. Report summarizing cross‐session reliability metrics."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Pre‐onset beta‐band (15–25 Hz) power suppression over auditory sensors is stronger for longer words (measured by character length).",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Test whether the magnitude of beta suppression before word onsets covaries with the length of the upcoming word’s orthographic form.",  
      "steps":"1. Load each session’s word events and compute word length as the number of characters in the 'segment' string. 2. For each word onset, epoch continuous MEG data from −0.3 to 0 s before onset. 3. Bandpass‐filter epochs in 15–25 Hz; compute mean power in each epoch and baseline‐correct by dividing by power in −0.3 to −0.25 s. 4. Select an auditory ROI (e.g., gradiometers over temporal sensors). Average power across ROI and time to get one suppression value per word. 5. Fit a linear regression predicting suppression magnitude from word‐length across all words in each session. 6. Aggregate regression slopes across sessions and test whether the mean slope is significantly negative via one‐sample t‐test across sessions.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Per‐session regression slopes and confidence intervals. 2. Group‐level t‐statistic and p‐value for mean slope < 0. 3. Scatter plots of word length vs. beta suppression with fitted lines for illustrative sessions. 4. Summary table of sample size, slope, and p‐values across sessions."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Post‐onset theta‐band (4–8 Hz) inter‐trial phase coherence (ITPC) in the auditory ROI is higher for vowel phonemes than for consonant phonemes.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Compare phase‐locking strength in the theta band following phoneme onset between vowels and consonants.",  
      "steps":"1. Normalize phoneme labels to ARPABET and classify each as vowel vs. consonant. 2. For each phoneme onset, epoch MEG data from 0 to 0.3 s post‐onset. 3. Bandpass filter epochs in 4–8 Hz and extract analytic phase via Hilbert transform. 4. For each time sample and channel in an auditory ROI, compute ITPC across all vowel epochs and separately across all consonant epochs. 5. Average ITPC over a window (e.g., 0.05–0.2 s) and across ROI channels to get one ITPC value per class per session. 6. Within each session, perform a paired permutation test (shuffle class labels across epochs, 1,000 iterations) to assess whether vowel‐ITPC > consonant‐ITPC.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Per‐session average ITPC curves (time × ROI) for vowels and consonants. 2. Session‐wise ITPC summary in 50–200 ms window and permutation p‐values. 3. Time‐series plots showing significant time points where vowel vs. consonant ITPC differs. 4. Consolidated report of effect sizes and significance across sessions."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Theta–gamma phase‐amplitude coupling (PAC) in the 0–200 ms window post‐word onset increases with word duration.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Assess whether longer words elicit stronger cross‐frequency coupling between theta phase and gamma amplitude immediately after word onset.",  
      "steps":"1. Extract word duration from each word event. 2. For each word, epoch MEG data from 0 to 0.2 s. 3. Bandpass‐filter epochs in theta (4–8 Hz) to extract instantaneous phase and in gamma (30–50 Hz) to extract amplitude envelope. 4. Compute PAC (e.g., modulation index) between theta phase and gamma amplitude for each epoch in an auditory ROI. 5. Fit a within‐session Spearman correlation between word duration and PAC values. 6. Aggregate session‐wise correlations and test if the median correlation is significantly positive across sessions via Wilcoxon signed‐rank test.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Per‐epoch PAC values annotated by word duration. 2. Per‐session correlation coefficients and p‐values. 3. Boxplots of PAC stratified by short vs. long words (e.g., median split). 4. Group‐level nonparametric test result with effect size."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Alpha‐band (8–12 Hz) power rebounds more strongly following long silence intervals (>0.5 s) than brief silences (<0.2 s).",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Determine whether the brain’s alpha rebound after periods of silence depends on silence duration.",  
      "steps":"1. From each session’s events.tsv, extract all silence segments (kind=='silence') and their durations. 2. Categorize silences as long (>0.5 s) or short (<0.2 s). 3. For each silence onset, epoch MEG data from 0 to 0.2 s post‐onset. 4. Bandpass‐filter epochs in 8–12 Hz; compute mean power in the rebound window (e.g., 0.05–0.15 s). 5. Average power across an occipital ROI (or all channels). 6. Within each session, compare mean alpha rebound between long vs. short silences using a paired t‐test (or nonparametric equivalent) across all epochs. 7. Correct p‐values across sessions (FDR).",  
      "deliverables":"1. Per‐session mean alpha rebound values for long and short silences. 2. Session‐wise t‐statistics, p‐values (raw and corrected), and Cohen’s d. 3. Violin plots of alpha rebound by silence category. 4. Summary table of results across sessions."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"ERF amplitudes in the 100–300 ms window at sentence‐initial words (wordidx=1) are larger than at sentence‐medial words (wordidx>1).",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Test whether the brain’s response to sentence‐initial words is enhanced relative to words in the middle of sentences.",  
      "steps":"1. From each session’s events.tsv, label word events as sentence‐initial (wordidx==1) or sentence‐medial (wordidx>1). 2. Epoch MEG data for each word from 0 to 0.3 s post‐onset, baseline‐correct on −0.1–0 s. 3. Define an ROI (e.g., all auditory gradiometers) and compute mean ERF amplitude in the 0.1–0.3 s window per epoch. 4. Compare amplitudes for sentence‐initial vs. sentence‐medial words within each session via paired testing (paired t‐test or Wilcoxon). 5. Pool across sessions in a mixed‐effects model with fixed effect for position (initial vs. medial) and random intercepts for session.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Per‐session mean ERF amplitudes for initial vs. medial words and test statistics. 2. Mixed‐effects model summary (fixed effect estimate, CI, p‐value). 3. Bar plots of group‐level amplitude by word position. 4. Report on sentence‐boundary processing effect."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Low‐frequency (<1 Hz) slope of ongoing MEG potentials within a sentence correlates positively with sentence length (number of words).",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Investigate whether longer sentences evoke a steeper build‐up of slow cortical potentials across their duration.",  
      "steps":"1. Group events by sentence (unique combination of chapter and sentenceidx). 2. For each sentence, extract MEG data from its first word onset to its last word onset + 0.5 s. 3. Low‐pass filter the continuous data at 1 Hz to isolate slow potentials. 4. For each sentence and each channel in a frontal‐ROI (or all channels), compute the linear slope of the potential over time via least‐squares fit. 5. Average slopes across channels to obtain one slow‐potential slope per sentence. 6. Record sentence length as total word count. 7. Across all sentences in each session and then pooled across sessions, test correlation between sentence length and slope using Spearman’s rho and assess significance via permutation of sentence labels.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Table of sentence lengths and their corresponding slow‐potential slopes. 2. Per‐session and pooled Spearman correlation coefficients and p‐values. 3. Scatter plot of slope vs. sentence length with fit line. 4. Permutation‐test histogram and p‐value."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Post‐onset gamma‐band (30–50 Hz) power is greater for first phonemes of words compared to last phonemes of words.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Assess whether the position of a phoneme within a word modulates the strength of its induced gamma response.",  
      "steps":"1. From each session’s events.tsv, for each word identify the first and last phoneme onsets (kind=='phoneme' with phonemeidx==1 and phonemeidx==max for that word). 2. For each selected phoneme onset, epoch MEG data from 0 to 0.2 s post‐onset. 3. Bandpass filter epochs in 30–50 Hz and compute mean power in 0.05–0.15 s window. 4. Average power across an auditory ROI. 5. Within each session, compare gamma power between first vs. last phonemes using paired t‐tests. 6. Adjust p‐values across sessions via FDR.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Per‐session average gamma power for first and last phoneme positions. 2. Session‐wise t‐statistics, raw and corrected p‐values, and effect sizes. 3. Boxplots of gamma power by phoneme position. 4. Summary interpretation of within‐word phoneme‐position effect."  
    }  
  }  
]}
